entals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "District of Columbia remains the most Democratic jurisdiction in presidential politics, and the open-seat contest to succeed the term-limited Republican president does nothing to change that. The district's partisan lean of roughly 44 points, paired with a national environment in which the president's approval is negative nearly everywhere, points to a comfortable Democratic victory. No public polling targets the district, but none is needed to identify the favorite.\n\nNo expert ratings, market prices, or fundraising figures are available, reflecting how little contest there is. The realistic question is the size of the Democratic margin, not which party wins. The district's heavy Democratic registration, large federal workforce, and consistent voting history all point the same direction, and the expected margin sits in the high forties.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9995, "margin": 44.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 1.0, "margin": 44.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 1.0, "margin": 50.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9999, "margin": 50.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 1.0, "margin": 50.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.9999, "margin": 52.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.9989, "margin": 44.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.9998, "margin": 50.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.9997, "margin": 50.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.9995, "margin": 47.0}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.9993, "margin": 44.0}, {"date": "2026-10-04", "p_dem": 0.9989, "margin": 45.0}, {"date": "2026-10-05", "p_dem": 0.9992, "margin": 45.0}, {"date": "2026-10-06", "p_dem": 0.9991, "margin": 46.0}, {"date": "2026-10-07", "p_dem": 0.9991, "margin": 44.0}, {"date": "2026-10-08", "p_dem": 0.9994, "margin": 44.0}, {"date": "2026-10-09", "p_dem": 0.9998, "margin": 44.0}, {"date": "2026-10-10", "p_dem": 1.0, "margin": 44.0}, {"d